{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"name":"python3","display_name":"Python 3","language":"python"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.10.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"colab":{"provenance":[],"authorship_tag":"ABX9TyPdq0wA/Anr2kzdDxV+VdEo"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":30746,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/jaiswalmagic1/mastering-regex?scriptVersionId=191098867\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **Regular expressions**","metadata":{"id":"VsFVjyfM8mQP"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **Introduction**","metadata":{"id":"k6Gz3J-W8vYH"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Regular expressions which are also called Res, RegEx or Regex patterns, are essentialy a tiny program inside Python and available through re module. Using this tiny module you create a pattern to match, search, split or modify a string.\n\nThese patterns are converted into a series of bytecodes that is executed by a matching engine written in c. For an advance use of RegEx or for optimization one needs to understand how a bytecode is executed by the matching engine.\n\nBecause this just a small launguage, RegEx also has limitations and not all task can be done using RegEx, because it makes the pattern very complicated and not readable. A python code mat slower but elaborate and readable for this purpose.","metadata":{"id":"QfwfAo_l8zMH"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **Matching Characters**","metadata":{"id":"Kx4ExezWALZd"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Most letters and charecters will simply match themselves. For examples the regular expression `test` will match the string 'test' exactly.\n\nThere are exceptions to this rule, some special metacharecters dont match themselves ad signle that some out of ordinary thing should be matched or they affect the other portion of the re by repeating or changing their meaning.\n\nHere is a complete list of metacharecter and their meaning.","metadata":{"id":"aOBXR6_eARNF"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <style>\n        table {\n            width: 100%;\n            border-collapse: collapse;\n            margin-top: 20px;\n            font-size: 1em; /* Increased font size by 1.5 times */\n        }\n        table, th, td {\n            border: 1px solid #ddd;\n        }\n        th, td {\n            padding: 10px;\n            text-align: left;\n        }\n        th {\n            background-color: #2c3e50;\n            color: white;\n        }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n<table>\n    <tr>\n        <th>Metacharacter</th>\n        <th>Meaning</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>^</td>\n        <td>Matches the start of a string.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>$</td>\n        <td>Matches the end of a string.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>*</td>\n        <td>Matches 0 or more repetitions of the preceding pattern.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>+</td>\n        <td>Matches 1 or more repetitions of the preceding pattern.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>?</td>\n        <td>Matches 0 or 1 repetition of the preceding pattern.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>{ }</td>\n        <td>Matches exactly n (or a range of) repetitions of the preceding pattern.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>[ ]</td>\n        <td>Matches any one of the characters within the brackets.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>\\</td>\n        <td>Escapes a metacharacter to match it literally.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>|</td>\n        <td>Acts as an OR operator between patterns.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>( )</td>\n        <td>Groups sub-patterns or captures matching text.</td>\n    </tr>\n</table>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n","metadata":{"id":"BkkRbGalDRl2"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **[ and ] - Charecter class**","metadata":{"id":"8b12s5m3EYTy"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"These are used to specify a charecter class, which are a set of charecters that one wishes to match. Charecters can be listed indivisually or a range of charecters can be indicated and separating them by '-'.\n\nfor example:\n[ abc ] will match any of the charecters 'a', 'b' or 'c'. This is same as [ a - c ] which uses a range to express the same set of charecters.\n\nOther metacharecters are not active inside the charecter classes. For example,  \\ is a charecter class but [ abc\\ ] will match 'a', 'b', 'c' or \\. Inside  charecter class \\ is stripped off its special nature.\n","metadata":{"id":"fd1TGV6cEaxh"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **[ ^ ] anchor in charecter class**","metadata":{"id":"jNFMRAt8LMFz"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"When '^' is supplied inside a charecter class, it acts as a negation operator.\n\nYou can match the charecters not listed in the within the class by complementing the set. This is indicated by including a '^' at the begining of the charecter class.\n\n[ ^5 ] will any charecter except '5', if the '^' appears anywhere else in the class, it will not have the special meaning. [ 5^ ] will match '5' or '^'.","metadata":{"id":"wrfOZuL_LQeY"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **\\ backslash**","metadata":{"id":"toXDrMqqOAeC"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"It is used to escape all the metacharecters so you can still match them in patterns.\n\nfor example, if you need to match `[` or `\\`, you can precede them with a backslash to remove their special meaning `\\[`, `\\\\`.\n\n\nSome of the special sequences beginning with `\\` represent predefined sets of characters that are often useful, such as the set of digits, the set of letters, or the set of anything that isn’t whitespace.","metadata":{"id":"Jm7chn_lOF60"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <style>\n        table {\n            width: 100%;\n            border-collapse: collapse;\n            margin-top: 20px;\n            font-size: 1.5em; /* Increased font size */\n        }\n        table, th, td {\n            border: 1px solid #ddd;\n        }\n        th, td {\n            padding: 10px;\n            text-align: left;\n        }\n        th {\n            background-color: #2c3e50;\n            color: white;\n        }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n<table>\n    <tr>\n        <th>Special Sequence</th>\n        <th>Meaning</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>\\d</td>\n        <td>Matches any decimal digit; equivalent to [0-9].</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>\\D</td>\n        <td>Matches any non-digit character; equivalent to [^0-9].</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>\\s</td>\n        <td>Matches any whitespace character; equivalent to [ \\t\\n\\r\\f\\v].</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>\\S</td>\n        <td>Matches any non-whitespace character; equivalent to [^ \\t\\n\\r\\f\\v].</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>\\w</td>\n        <td>Matches any alphanumeric character; equivalent to [a-zA-Z0-9_].</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>\\W</td>\n        <td>Matches any non-alphanumeric character; equivalent to [^a-zA-Z0-9_].</td>\n    </tr>\n</table>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n","metadata":{"id":"NNMHqVj4QE6e"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re-syntax","metadata":{"id":"PO-JpRHZww7B"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **. dot**","metadata":{"id":"uyTDIQ7XwAQl"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The final metacharacter in this section is .. It matches anything except a newline character, and there’s an alternate mode (re.DOTALL) where it will match even a newline. . is often used where you want to match “any character”.","metadata":{"id":"wThsvCn_wD3z"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **`|` Alternation, or the “or” operator**","metadata":{"id":"j7me0SeENt6P"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"If A and B are regular expressions, A|B will match any string that matches either A or B. | has very low precedence in order to make it work reasonably when you’re alternating multi-character strings. Crow|Servo will match either 'Crow' or 'Servo', not 'Cro', a 'w' or an 'S', and 'ervo'.\n\nTo match a literal '|', use \\|, or enclose it inside a character class, as in [|].","metadata":{"id":"IsHX-wVFNxlN"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **^** Caret","metadata":{"id":"pLzNtDr_OgZ-"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Matches at the beginning of lines. Unless the MULTILINE flag has been set, this will only match at the beginning of the string. In MULTILINE mode, this also matches immediately after each newline within the string.\n\nFor example, if you wish to match the word From only at the beginning of a line, the RE to use is ^From.\n","metadata":{"id":"hhS_0eydOjw0"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(re.search('^From', 'From Here to Eternity'))","metadata":{"id":"ZXw2K3BiOveh","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722470371235,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":661,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"71027f0c-9b46-493e-9340-4c4bc179376a"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(re.search('^From', 'Reciting From Memory'))","metadata":{"id":"5KdF9p_DO0Yl","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722470359105,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":866,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"38efcde6-85d5-423a-a972-5cfb31dbf083"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"To match a literal '^', use \\^.","metadata":{"id":"99cp_Ar5Ox71"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **$ End of line**","metadata":{"id":"IQSu5WwwPLop"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Matches at the end of a line, which is defined as either the end of the string, or any location followed by a newline character.","metadata":{"id":"7BfSwiM4PPT9"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(re.search('}$', '{block}'))","metadata":{"id":"klGOPlyFPUfc","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722470511915,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":615,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"235e9a33-8e5a-4349-bb4e-41e206295a38"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Here, the string {block} ends with }, so the pattern matches.","metadata":{"id":"_OA_nzDEQsTi"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(re.search('}$', '{block} '))","metadata":{"id":"3jIakFw-PjoD","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722470524499,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":531,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"70a497a5-0b6e-4821-be0b-21c45fb215bc"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Here, the string {block} ends with a space after }, so the pattern does not match.","metadata":{"id":"lGI0Pws3QzWP"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(re.search('}$', '{block}\\n'))","metadata":{"id":"szUSmRoHPnJ3","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722470572544,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":576,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"c3c05c75-7bc9-403b-b5b3-7ed299542d3f"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Here, the string {block}\\n has } followed by a newline character, so the pattern matches.","metadata":{"id":"5yI_q4tAQ2WU"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"To match a literal `'$'`, use `\\$` or enclose it inside a character class, as in `[$]`.","metadata":{"id":"MywrLwyJP1QG"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **\\A**","metadata":{"id":"IXrHsVRDRLYe"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Matches only at the start of the string. When not in MULTILINE mode, \\A and ^ are effectively the same. In MULTILINE mode, they’re different: \\A still matches only at the beginning of the string, but ^ may match at any location inside the string that follows a newline character.","metadata":{"id":"FfD2JsvbRQHW"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **\\Z**","metadata":{"id":"JIJnLEASRTCi"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Matches only at the end of the string.","metadata":{"id":"bwIq7tJiRXYl"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **\\b**","metadata":{"id":"Xzo8e-APRYht"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Word boundary. This is a zero-width assertion that matches only at the beginning or end of a word. A word is defined as a sequence of alphanumeric characters, so the end of a word is indicated by whitespace or a non-alphanumeric character.\n\n\nThe following example matches class only when it’s a complete word; it won’t match when it’s contained inside another word.","metadata":{"id":"JxlFtTX0SUTD"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\np = re.compile(r'\\bclass\\b')\nprint(p.search('no class at all'))","metadata":{"id":"eKo9G5tCSWdW","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722471277326,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":571,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"7704f740-f421-40b5-b761-66f47e92d566"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(p.search('the declassified algorithm'))","metadata":{"id":"hK1KUym1ShbK","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722471302826,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":562,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"d6d5b20f-c84f-456a-db16-58b9aaf81791"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(p.search('one subclass is'))","metadata":{"id":"75jfC08gSmoD","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722471318103,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":546,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"2cc7bd5b-2249-435b-a087-116ebc654ef5"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"There are two subtleties you should remember when using this special sequence. First, this is the worst collision between Python’s string literals and regular expression sequences. In Python’s string literals, \\b is the backspace character, ASCII value 8. If you’re not using raw strings, then Python will convert the \\b to a backspace, and your RE won’t match as you expect it to. The following example looks the same as our previous RE, but omits the 'r' in front of the RE string.","metadata":{"id":"NASZZgamSvlg"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\np = re.compile('\\bclass\\b')\nprint(p.search('no class at all'))","metadata":{"id":"-WHQVRt2Syjr","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722471373973,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":555,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"40ff32c3-152b-4989-eed1-9703db806b57"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nprint(p.search('\\b' + 'class' + '\\b'))","metadata":{"id":"HPvN-u4lS3uX","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722471387549,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":530,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"28c0bf17-a034-43ea-998e-daf21172b5e6"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Second, inside a character class, where there’s no use for this assertion, \\b represents the backspace character, for compatibility with Python’s string literals.","metadata":{"id":"usWM9grJS7cH"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **\\B**","metadata":{"id":"iAeA7H_7TfUr"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Another zero-width assertion, this is the opposite of \\b, only matching when the current position is not at a word boundary.","metadata":{"id":"cjzjD74TTjAT"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **Repeating Things**\n\n\n","metadata":{"id":"JV3nW3QfxpC8"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Another capability of regex is that you can specify that portions of the RE must be repeated a certain number of times.","metadata":{"id":"C7Svybupx1Di"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### *** quantifier**","metadata":{"id":"M18Mn0RvyUZ9"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`*` does not match the literal character '*'; instead, it specifies that the previous character can be matched zero or more times, instead of exactly once.\n\nFor example, ca*t will match 'ct' (0 'a' characters), 'cat' (1 'a'), 'caaat' (3 'a' characters), and so forth.\n\nRepetitions such as * are greedy; when repeating a RE, the matching engine will try to repeat it as many times as possible. If later portions of the pattern don’t match, the matching engine will then back up and try again with fewer repetitions.","metadata":{"id":"c34nF07ryoar"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **+ quantifier**","metadata":{"id":"WFRPY_ko_-El"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`*` matches zero or more times, so whatever’s being repeated may not be present at all, while\n\n`+` requires at least one occurrence.\n\nTo use a similar example, ca+t will match 'cat' (1 'a'), 'caaat' (3 'a's), but won’t match 'ct'.","metadata":{"id":"M8lMst9vAHmu"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **? quantifier**","metadata":{"id":"0fubGz7IAm6j"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`?` matches either once or zero times; you can think of it as marking something as being optional.\n\nFor example, home-?brew matches either 'homebrew' or 'home-brew'.","metadata":{"id":"OFrHGaB3AsfV"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **{m,n} quantifier**","metadata":{"id":"7G_wwg5qA1bd"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The most complicated quantifier is {m,n}, where m and n are decimal integers. This quantifier means there must be at least m repetitions, and at most n. For example, a/{1,3}b will match 'a/b', 'a//b', and 'a///b'. It won’t match 'ab', which has no slashes, or 'a////b', which has four.\n\n\nYou can omit either m or n; in that case, a reasonable value is assumed for the missing value. Omitting m is interpreted as a lower limit of 0, while omitting n results in an upper bound of infinity.\n\n\nThe simplest case {m} matches the preceding item exactly m times. For example, a/{2}b will only match 'a//b'.\n\n\nReaders of a reductionist bent may notice that the three other quantifiers can all be expressed using this notation. {0,} is the same as *, {1,} is equivalent to +, and {0,1} is the same as ?. It’s better to use *, +, or ? when you can, simply because they’re shorter and easier to read.","metadata":{"id":"hQWtTK04BFSa"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **Grouping**","metadata":{"id":"mr0FkqspUkQp"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Groups are marked by the '(', ')' metacharacters. '(' and ')' have much the same meaning as they do in mathematical expressions; they group together the expressions contained inside them, and you can repeat the contents of a group with a quantifier, such as *, +, ?, or {m,n}. For example, (ab)* will match zero or more repetitions of ab.","metadata":{"id":"vHuNz_ICUuJT"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\np = re.compile('(ab)*')\nprint(p.match('ababababab').span())","metadata":{"id":"_1bJ43N8Uwp2","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722471891216,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":925,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"61851cb3-0a00-480f-fbfd-65e4241ef81b"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Groups indicated with '(', ')' also capture the starting and ending index of the text that they match; this can be retrieved by passing an argument to group(), start(), end(), and span(). Groups are numbered starting with 0. Group 0 is always present; it’s the whole RE, so match object methods all have group 0 as their default argument.","metadata":{"id":"boAfeEhgmo35"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\np = re.compile('(a)b')\nm = p.match('ab')\nm.group()\n\nm.group(0)","metadata":{"id":"cD0IvztKnQ89","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722644540917,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":484,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"7e25c5ec-4b23-42c2-a814-821b44d7befc"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Subgroups are numbered from left to right, from 1 upward. Groups can be nested; to determine the number, just count the opening parenthesis characters, going from left to right.","metadata":{"id":"E9AJQenlnsj9"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\np = re.compile('(a(b)c)d')\nm = p.match('abcd')\nm.group(0)","metadata":{"id":"psZXALhfntg3","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722644659888,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":493,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"91996c3b-f79e-491c-d204-d03873eac8ea"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"m.group(1)","metadata":{"id":"aFm242Ahnz2f","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722644661718,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":23,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"cc9e5213-39a8-4b93-ac66-97f79147b96e"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"m.group(2)","metadata":{"id":"rbVzzOAqn1iP","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722644664219,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":471,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"0385ec29-783a-4966-a095-705ffbdd0bd7"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"group() can be passed multiple group numbers at a time, in which case it will return a tuple containing the corresponding values for those groups.","metadata":{"id":"2PfNf5FAn-LZ"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"m.group(2,1,2)","metadata":{"id":"WJyE37mdn_EV","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722644699589,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":473,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"237eb675-77bf-4933-a89a-6f391a8661d7"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The groups() method returns a tuple containing the strings for all the subgroups, from 1 up to however many there are.","metadata":{"id":"PvOa_y7OoN3u"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"m.groups()","metadata":{"id":"Rj3yvUefoNAJ","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722644758776,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":448,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"75c8bc3f-6075-4c5a-c3b3-8c557032a9b6"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **Compiling Regular Expressions**","metadata":{"id":"UOarT-v-EQbc"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Regular expressions are compiled into pattern objects, which have methods for various operations such as searching for pattern matches or performing string substitutions.","metadata":{"id":"-kfLw3YsEbQg"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\np = re.compile('ab*')","metadata":{"id":"meUxOeCRElMV"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"p","metadata":{"id":"dEHdyGr7Erah","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722434119523,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":1841,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"a5ed129a-99e5-4980-9262-4efea93d0631"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"re.compile() also accepts an optional flags argument, used to enable various special features and syntax variations.","metadata":{"id":"K5KRWNfaKcJC"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **The Backslash Plague**","metadata":{"id":"HWL55IRCK520"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Regular expressions (regex) use backslashes (\\) to denote special characters or forms, conflicting with Python's use of backslashes in string literals. To match \\section in a LaTeX file, you must escape backslashes, resulting in \\\\section. In Python, this becomes \"\\\\\\\\section\".\n\n\nStages:\n\nText string to be matched: \\section\n\nEscaped for re.compile(): \\\\section\n\nEscaped for Python string literal: \"\\\\\\\\section\"\n\nTo avoid this complexity, use Python’s raw string notation (r\"\"), where backslashes are treated literally:\n\n\nRegular String: \"\\\\section\"\n\nRaw String: r\"\\\\section\"\n\nRaw strings make regex patterns easier to read and write.","metadata":{"id":"yo2R7LgRK-D6"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **Performing Matches**","metadata":{"id":"fX6BUkEWMige"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Once you have an object representing a compiled regular expression, what do you do with it? Pattern objects have several methods and attributes.","metadata":{"id":"lA7Yha7-Mkhd"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <style>\n        table {\n            width: 100%;\n            border-collapse: collapse;\n            margin-top: 20px;\n            font-size: 1.5em; /* Increased font size */\n        }\n        table, th, td {\n            border: 1px solid #ddd;\n        }\n        th, td {\n            padding: 10px;\n            text-align: left;\n        }\n        th {\n            background-color: #2c3e50;\n            color: white;\n        }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n<table>\n    <tr>\n        <th>Method/Attribute</th>\n        <th>Purpose</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>match()</td>\n        <td>Determine if the RE matches at the beginning of the string.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>search()</td>\n        <td>Scan through a string, looking for any location where this RE matches.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>findall()</td>\n        <td>Find all substrings where the RE matches, and returns them as a list.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>finditer()</td>\n        <td>Find all substrings where the RE matches, and returns them as an iterator.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>group()</td>\n        <td>Return the string matched by the RE.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>start()</td>\n        <td>Return the starting position of the match.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>end()</td>\n        <td>Return the ending position of the match.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>span()</td>\n        <td>Return a tuple containing the (start, end) positions of the match.</td>\n    </tr>\n</table>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n","metadata":{"id":"q8N4kx8uNEvl"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **Modifying Strings**","metadata":{"id":"0f-gRVxBqInm"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Regular expressions are also commonly used to modify strings in various ways, using the following pattern methods","metadata":{"id":"QU-0QbVpqOjG"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <style>\n        table {\n            width: 100%;\n            border-collapse: collapse;\n            margin-top: 20px;\n            font-size: 1.5em; /* Increased font size */\n        }\n        table, th, td {\n            border: 1px solid #ddd;\n        }\n        th, td {\n            padding: 10px;\n            text-align: left;\n        }\n        th {\n            background-color: #2c3e50;\n            color: white;\n        }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n<table>\n    <tr>\n        <th>Method/Attribute</th>\n        <th>Purpose</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>split()</td>\n        <td>Split the string into a list, splitting it wherever the RE matches.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>sub()</td>\n        <td>Find all substrings where the RE matches, and replace them with a different string.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>subn()</td>\n        <td>Does the same thing as sub(), but returns the new string and the number of replacements.</td>\n    </tr>\n</table>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n","metadata":{"id":"EK_rLwrHqeGt"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## **Practice**","metadata":{"id":"HWxFYfDVspY8"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re","metadata":{"id":"9QsMThHPsoID","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722646005710,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":505,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"text = '''Companies contact number is 9999999999, 8888888888 and it's US contact number is (999)-333-7777'''","metadata":{"id":"ht-5VeC5tAfp","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722646403337,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":488,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Extracting Indian Phone Number**","metadata":{"id":"kGOxyPN0u-qr"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pattern = '\\d{10}'\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"ZkFtyYLstV9L","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722646405146,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":13,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"c182f09d-ade6-47c3-ee56-4196cee0045b"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Extracting US Phone Number**","metadata":{"id":"G-GT9EnQvHQa"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pattern = '\\(\\d{3}\\)-\\d{3}-\\d{4}'\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"vlehhYEwuFP9","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722646504444,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":554,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"cfd9102d-3c22-43ae-f0ec-89add5e3fc59"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Extracting Indian and US Phone Number**","metadata":{"id":"cs9kEJVRvM2q"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pattern = '\\d{10}|\\(\\d{3}\\)-\\d{3}-\\d{4}'\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"khEMGFLIvRLj","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722646629783,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":676,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"12644003-ccfe-4db7-8c7f-fb701191491e"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Extracting titles**","metadata":{"id":"lU_njHAbwHLJ"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"text = '''Note 1 Overview\nThe company, established in 2005, has grown to become a leading player in the tech industry. With a strong commitment to innovation and customer satisfaction, we have expanded our product offerings and market presence globally. Our mission is to deliver high-quality technology solutions that drive business success for our clients.\nNote 2 Overview of Significant Accounting Policies\nRevenue Recognition\nRevenue is recognized when control of the promised goods or services is transferred to customers, in an amount that reflects the consideration to which the company expects to be entitled in exchange for those goods or services. The company applies a five-step model to determine when to recognize revenue and at what amount.\nInventory Valuation\nInventories are valued at the lower of cost or net realizable value. Cost is determined using the first-in, first-out (FIFO) method. Net realizable value is the estimated selling price in the ordinary course of business, less applicable variable selling expenses.\nDepreciation\nProperty, plant, and equipment are recorded at cost and depreciated over their estimated useful lives using the straight-line method. Depreciation expense is recognized in the income statement as part of operating expenses.\nFinancial Instruments\nThe company classifies its financial instruments into three categories: financial assets at fair value through profit or loss, financial assets at amortized cost, and financial liabilities. Initial recognition is at fair value, with subsequent measurement depending on the classification.\n '''","metadata":{"id":"EP3aCI7DwIR-","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722646887993,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":469,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pattern = 'Note \\d [^\\n]*' # [^\\n]* means match evryithing which is not a new line so when it matches a new line it stops.\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"ob7jKt3Nwquj","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722647188452,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":459,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"f81f4055-4087-4929-e181-e7a56b193c8a"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pattern = 'Note \\d ([^\\n]*)'\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"DqvqgUKHy7KH","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722647569369,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":599,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"34f5b7cc-6350-4091-c904-f4e8d5b6795f"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Extracting the financial periods from a companies financial reporting**","metadata":{"id":"0DC7A1jq0Q9s"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"text = ''' the gross cost of operating lease vehicles in FY2021 Q1 was $4.85 billion.\nIn the previous quarter i.e. FY2020 Q4 it was $3 billion. '''\n\npattern = 'FY\\d{4} Q[1-4]'\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"63K0q2FX023u","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722648230920,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":524,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"d1824264-c20f-4bae-c7c2-fd77e6c91c03"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Case sensetive pattern match using flags**","metadata":{"id":"HdKTAGNK1j9Y"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"text = ''' the gross cost of operating lease vehicles in FY2021 Q1 was $4.85 billion.\nIn the previous quarter i.e. fy2020 Q4 it was $3 billion. '''\n\npattern = 'FY\\d{4} Q[1-4]'\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text, flags=re.IGNORECASE)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"d51VRDAL1r9J","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722648345390,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":451,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"48ed1796-d850-4e18-f659-bbc4b61df99b"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Extracting only Year and Quarter**","metadata":{"id":"MPIeqENr2G7i"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"text = ''' the gross cost of operating lease vehicles in FY2021 Q1 was $4.85 billion.\nIn the previous quarter i.e. fy2020 Q4 it was $3 billion. '''\n\npattern = 'FY(\\d{4} Q[1-4])'\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, text, flags=re.IGNORECASE)\nprint(matches)","metadata":{"id":"KHi6F6u_2Otw","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722648432787,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":451,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"outputId":"d998ff00-cab6-46f9-93f9-5b4fca83f37b"},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Using RegEx on Pandas Dataframe**","metadata":{"id":"2zAg3s4u2gcY"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import pandas as pd\nimport re","metadata":{"id":"0WjRxJVa23zn","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1722648603866,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":1354,"user":{"displayName":"Vishal Jaiswal","userId":"10983234184945489809"}},"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"202